Let a keybind choose where its new tab goes: new_tab:end, new_tab:current

Where a new tab opens came only from window-new-tab-position, for every
way of opening one. A key that should open its tab at the end of the
list while Cmd-T keeps opening next to the current tab could not be
written. Chaining move_tab after new_tab does not help: move_tab acts on
the tab that is still selected, which is the old one.

new_tab now takes an optional argument, the same way close_tab does:
- new_tab, or new_tab:config, places the tab where
  window-new-tab-position says, as before
- new_tab:current places it right after the current tab
- new_tab:end places it at the end of the tab list

The value travels through the apprt action (apprt.action.NewTabPosition)
and the C API (ghostty_action_new_tab_position_e, union member
new_tab_position) to the macOS app, which hands TerminalController a
window-new-tab-position value ("current" or "end"), or nothing for
config. GTK places the tab the same way. Opening a tab any other way
(the tab bar's + button, the dock, AppleScript, App Intents) still
follows the config.

A binding to new_tab now prints as new_tab:config in +list-keybinds and
+show-config, as close_tab prints as close_tab:this. Bare new_tab and
new_tab:config are one action, so the New Tab menu item still shows the
key bound to new_tab, and a key bound to new_tab:end never takes its
place.

